# Milestone: M3
# Covers: when all strands are on the sleep queue (none on the ready queue),
#         sched_quiescent returns false (wakeup_pending_count > 0); after the
#         timer fires and strands are moved back to ready, quiescent stays false
#         until they finish, then becomes true.
#
# blocked: post-completion QUIESCENT transition not observable via the host driver
# matrix-row: scheduler-quiescent
#
# UPDATE (v1.0 / M10): the lone-sleeper WAKE is now fixed (design-risks
# v0.11.4-A) — sched_wake_due_sleepers runs as a pre-dispatch pump in urbi_step,
# so an expired sleeper resumes (see the now-active wait_sleep_basic.chk and
# temporal/sleep_tag_stop.chk).  This fixture stays blocked on a DIFFERENT,
# pre-existing concern: observing the transition BACK to QUIESCENT after the
# sleeper completes.  Under the persistent-loader-strand model a WAITING strand
# is intentionally counted in strand_runnable_count (sched_post_dispatch step 1,
# "so the scheduler does not lose track of a waiting strand"), so urbi_step
# returns RUNNING (not WAKE_AT/QUIESCENT) while any strand is parked, and the
# multi-line host driver accumulates loader strands across `## host: run` lines.
# Asserting the precise QUIESCENT edge therefore needs loader-count-quiescence
# reconciliation in the host driver, tracked as a v1.x design-risk (not the wake
# gap).  The quiescence counter semantics themselves are unit-tested directly via
# the scheduler API (test_step_driver.c, test_scheduler_invariant.c).
